WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.520
Let’s say you’re trying to
find some solid information

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.980
about a serious health
problem that concerns you,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.640
like high blood pressure,
or diabetes.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.280
Yet, everywhere you look,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.040
someone’s trying to sell you
something like vitamins,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.280
yoga mats, blenders, drugs.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.880
Well, breathe a
sigh of relief

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.000
because all we bring
you are the facts.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.400
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.880
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.840
Today we talk about the best way to
manage the symptoms of menopause,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.680
starting with the management
of genitourinary symptoms.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.160
Typically starting about four
to five years after their last period,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.640
about half of postmenopausal women
suffer from what we used to call

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.840
vulvovaginal atrophy,
now referred to by the name

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.960
GSM, genitourinary syndrome
of menopause,

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.880
thanks to the Vulvovaginal Atrophy
Terminology Consensus Conference

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.920
who needed a more
“publicly acceptable term.”

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.440
After all, the word atrophy
had “negative connotations,”

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.800
and the word vagina “is not a generally
accepted term for public discourse.”

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.120
They liken it to the shift
from the “pejorative”

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.960
impotence to “erectile dysfunction.”

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.520
Whatever you call it,
it involves changes to the vulva

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.880
(the external genitalia), the vagina
(the birth canal), and bladder,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.240
caused by menopausal
changes in hormone levels.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.240
Vaginal symptoms include vaginal
dryness, burning, itchiness

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.200
and irritation, pain
during penetrative sex,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.520
and post-coital bleeding

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.360
from the thinning
of the vaginal lining.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.640
Urinary symptoms can include recurrent
bladder infections and incontinence.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.600
Some women with mild GSM
remain asymptomatic.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.440
For others, symptom severity
can preclude intercourse altogether

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.720
and result in discomfort
even with just sitting or wiping.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.200
In a survey of thousands
of women with GSM,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.120
59 percent said their symptoms

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.680
“considerably decreased
their enjoyment

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.440
of sexual activity,” and 23 percent
reported having

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.160
an adverse effect on their
“general enjoyment of life.”

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.480
While other menopausal symptoms
like hot flashes

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.040
tend to improve over time,

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.560
GSM symptoms tend to get
progressively worse.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.800
Unfortunately, women
rarely seek medical help,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.480
even though there are safe
and simple treatments available.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.720
The first-line treatment for mild
to moderate vaginal dryness

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.280
are lubricants and moisturizers.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.840
Lubricants are designed to reduce
friction during sexual activity,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.800
whereas vaginal moisturizers
are used on a regular basis

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.000
(daily or every two
to three days as needed)

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.720
to provide day-to-day
comfort by mimicking

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.040
normal vaginal secretions
regardless of sexual activity.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.040
Water-based lubricants

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.760
have the advantage
of being non-staining

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.880
and are associated
with fewer genital symptoms

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.360
such as discomfort or burning,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.720
compared to silicone-
based lubricants.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.320
What’s the best vaginal moisturizer?

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.800
This head-to-head study pitted
vaginal estrogen versus Replens,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.640
an expensive vaginal moisturizer

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.320
claiming special
“bioadhesive” ingredients,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.440
versus a placebo gel
of hydroxyethylcellulose,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.960
which is found in products
15 times cheaper like K-Y Jelly.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.520
After 12 weeks, the researchers
found no difference

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.840
between any of them—
they all worked the same.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.760
This “striking
double-negative finding”

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.560
led to an accompanying commentary

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.000
in the American Medical Association
journal to conclude

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.920
that until there is evidence
to suggest otherwise,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.880
“postmenopausal women
experiencing vulvovaginal symptoms

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.760
should choose the cheapest
moisturizer or lubricant

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.240
available over the counter”
instead of wasting your money.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.680
That was for efficacy, though.
What about safety?

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.720
The World Health Organization
recommends, based on the SMI test,

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.960
that personal lubricants
and vaginal moisturizers

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.000
not exceed an osmolality of 380.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.840
That’s a measure of how concentrated
the dissolved components are.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.360
How did they come up
with that number?

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.160
By lubing up slugs.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.160
SMI stands for slug
mucosal irritation.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.800
They cover slugs with lubricant
over a five-day period

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.480
and measure how much
mucosal irritation

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.080
and tissue damage
the slug experiences.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.760
No adverse effects
below the 380 cut-off,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.440
but something like K-Y Jelly
at over 2,000

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.480
induced mild to moderate irritation,
and something off the charts

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:18.800
like Astroglide at 5,000+ caused severe
irritation and tissue damage.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.360
Dozens of commonly used lubricants
and moisturizers available world-wide

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.400
have been put to the test,
and the only two vaginal moisturizers

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.880
that met the WHO criteria
were the Yes brand

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.840
aloe vera gel-based moisturizer

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.400
and the Balance Activ brand
hyaluronic acid-based one.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.040
The only lubricants that made
the cut were those made

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.360
by brands Yes, Good Clean Love,
System Jo, and one product by Durex,

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.600
their Sensilube gel, but not
their Play Feel lubricant.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:49.520
In our next story,

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.287
we find out if vaginal estrogen carries
the same risk as oral estrogen.

00:04:55.287 --> 00:04:56.280
The first-line treatment

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.200
for mild to moderate vaginal dryness
due to menopause are lubricants

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.360
and moisturizers.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.720
If over-the-counter lubricants
and moisturizers are insufficient

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.480
to control the genitourinary
symptoms of menopause, low-dose,

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.080
local estrogen therapy
is recommended,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.920
unless women have a history
of hormone-dependent cancers

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.000
like endometrial or breast.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.160
Local, meaning applied vaginally,
as opposed to taken orally.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.040
Vaginal application is considered
safer and more effective

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.160
than systemic hormone therapy.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.080
A meta-analysis of 58 studies
comparing vaginal

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.960
to systemic estrogens
found that vaginal estrogen therapy

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.000
offered better symptom relief
than estrogen pills, patches, or implants.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.040
In fact, many women who are on systemic
menopausal hormone therapy

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:44.880
have to add on supplemental vaginal
estrogens to control symptoms.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.680
Vaginal estrogens are available
as a variety of creams,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.520
suppositories, and rings.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.760
Thirty randomized, control comparative
trials have been performed,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.360
and there appears to be
no difference in efficacy

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.640
between the various preparations.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.000
However, they may take weeks
before a noticeable alleviation

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.480
of symptoms is detected,
and two to three months

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.040
before the full effect is achieved.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.720
Although year-long studies
can clearly demonstrate

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.040
vaginal estrogen’s benefit,
studies as long as 12 weeks

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.920
have failed to manifest
superiority to placebo.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.440
Some of the estrogen applied
to the vulva or vagina

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.400
is systemically absorbed
and therefore convey

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.320
the same black box FDA notice
that oral estrogens carry,

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.440
an all-caps warning of increased risk
of “endometrial cancer,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.040
cardiovascular disorders, breast cancer
and probable dementia.”

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.640
Vaginal estrogen
is considered safer, though,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.160
since it can be used
at a much lower dose

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.160
(as low as one-hundredth the oral dose).

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.600
The Harvard Nurse’s Health Study
did not find any increased risks

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.680
associated with vaginal estrogen use
over 18 years of follow-up.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.920
Randomized controlled trials
lasting up to a year

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.920
appear to confirm its safety,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.760
but there have been observational
studies linking vaginal use

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.840
to about a doubling of odds
for endometrial cancer,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.000
but this was done
back in the 1970s

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.080
when higher estrogen doses
were used.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.040
And a more recent study out of Denmark
that found the same thing

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.000
may have been confounded
by concurrent oral estrogen exposure.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.360
Out of an abundance of caution, though,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.080
even low-dose localized estrogen
may be contraindicated

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.960
in hormone-dependent cancer survivors
to be on the safe side.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.400
Breast cancer survivors
suffering from GSM

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.240
may want to consider
vaginal DHEA instead.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.880
Oral DHEA doesn’t appear
to offer any benefit,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.320
but in 2016 the FDA approved vaginal
DHEA suppositories for pain

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.880
during intercourse due to menopause.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.160
It’s converted locally into estrogen

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.920
and does not significantly
affect systemic hormone levels.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.160
A downside is that it has to be
administered nightly,

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.680
whereas estrogen preparations
are typically twice a week,

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.920
or even every few months
with the vaginal rings.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.400
For those who would rather
have an oral treatment,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.360
there is ospemifene,
a tamoxifen-type drug

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.120
that has pro-estrogenic effects
on the vaginal lining.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.640
However, it can actually double
the rate of hot flashes

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.080
and urinary tract infections
in the short-term,

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.760
and insufficient data is available
for long-term safety.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:15.400
Did you know that soy foods may
explain why Japanese-American women

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:19.200
not only have the lowest rates
of hot flashes in the United States

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.640
but also have the lowest
rates of vaginal dryness.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:23.959
Here’s the story.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.480
One consensus panel
of experts concluded

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.640
that soy can be considered
a first-line treatment

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.760
for menopausal hot flash
and night sweat symptoms.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.360
What about for vaginal dryness?

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.680
There have been a few studies
on the topical application

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.160
of soy isoflavone vaginal gels,
which in general have shown

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.920
beneficial effects, for example,
showing a significant improvement

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:46.000
in dryness and pain-with-intercourse
over placebo gels.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.120
In fact, they’re roughly
on par with estrogen cream

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:50.680
in a head-to-head test.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.280
But what about just eating soy?

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.480
Soy isoflavones improve female
sexual dysfunction of mice,

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.840
significantly increasing
vaginal blood flow,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.120
but what about in people?

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.600
The data using isolated soy
supplements is mixed.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.880
Some studies totally flopped,
showing no benefit over placebo,

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.440
all the way up to studies
showing comparable efficacy

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.040
when pitted head-to-head
against hormone therapy

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.440
for both hot flashes and muscle pain,
as well as vaginal dryness,

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.520
with no significant differences
between the two.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.040
And of course, soy has the benefit
of “no increased risk of breast

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.560
and uterine cancer
or cardiovascular disease,”

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:30.760
unlike systemic hormonal
menopause therapy.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.120
What about soy foods, though,
rather than soy supplements?

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.320
Well, there was a muffin study.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.160
Women randomized
to daily muffins

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.360
containing soy flour
or ground flaxseeds

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.280
saw no difference
in menopausal sexual symptoms

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.000
compared to wheat
flour placebo muffins.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.160
Similarly, women randomized
to 12 weeks of a daily scoop

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.520
of soy protein powder experienced
a significant improvement

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.400
in sexual quality
of life symptoms,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.680
but not compared
to the milk protein control group.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.120
What about soymilk?
There have been three studies.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.520
Compared to women
randomized to drink

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.520
about two cups of dairy milk
a day for eight months,

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.920
those randomized to soymilk
ended up with significantly fewer

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.920
menopausal sexual symptoms,
though this appeared to be more

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.840
due to symptoms
getting worse on the dairy

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.240
than getting better on the soy.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.840
That reminds me of a study
in Thailand where women

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.880
randomized to a soy-free diet
experienced a worsening

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.920
of vaginal dryness and overactive
bladder-type symptoms,

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:28.280
but adding extra soy didn’t seem
to make them better.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.600
The second soymilk study
found about two cups a day

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.560
over two months appeared to drop
vaginal and sexual symptoms

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.200
by a whopping 60 to 70 percent

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.360
compared to the do-nothing
control group,

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.640
but since the control group
didn’t drink anything special,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.840
it’s impossible to rule out
placebo effects.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.440
The latest study found significant
improvements in sexual symptoms

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:52.040
after just six weeks of less than
a cup of soymilk a day,

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.680
but again, the control group
did nothing.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.800
So, does soymilk work?

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:00.200
We really don’t know,
but it can’t hurt to give it a try—

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.720
unless you overdo it.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.200
A 44-year-old New Yorker
presented to her gynecologist

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.520
with an “increase in desire
that required her to self-stimulate

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.780
to orgasm
approximately 15 times daily.”

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.280
It turns out, a month
before she started eating

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:19.760
in excess of four pounds
of soy foods a day.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.440
Thankfully, within three months
of cutting back,

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.320
her desire cooled
to the point that she was able

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:28.360
to “engage in satisfying sexual activity
only twice daily.”

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:30.600
